ons or  tens of millions we need some sort of  signal that tells us what's going on in  the economy it turns out in economics we  get that signal in market societies and  it's in the form of prices I think of  like a coca-cola say using aluminum cans  it might be that somewhere on the other  side of the world there's a disaster in  the mine and then it's a little bit  harder to mine aluminium and the price  of aluminium will go up a little bit  because people compete to buy it and  then it comes to coca-cola and they say  well aluminium is a little more  expensive we don't even know why but  we'll just switch over to using plastic  bottles and use less aluminium and then  the price will change as a result of  that and we're all making all these  private decisions and it modifies prices  a little bit and then we respond  appropriately we don't know what's  causing scarcity we don't know what  other people's desires our demands are  we can just see that the price of  strawberries is cheap over here and it's  expensive over here and that tells me  everything I need to know is the  consumer about what to do with the  strawberries in order to best satisfy  other people's needs the problem with  massive socialism socialism a mass scale  is that they don't have a substitute for  prices so socialist planners have to  basically guess and that's why that's  why socialism on the Mount large scale  never ends up working because the  socialist planners literally don't have  the information  they need to make rational economic  decisions and I think that's really  important because in Washington DC I do  not think that there is a cabal of  closeted socialists that want to bring  about a marxist revolution but there is  a very prevalent particularly among the  elite political punting class like a  kind of light maybe less dangerous  version of the socialist conceit which  is a lot of these pundits have ample  number of Ivy League degrees and they  hang around a lot of other smart 