utes  and advantages at birth and by genetics  and by environment and someone with  maybe more progressive politics someone  who's a bit more friendly to the notion  of government intrusion oftentimes  that's based in an authentic and  good-hearted desire to correct for those  particular kind of arbitrary  distributions of advantages you know I'm  not really concerned about a quality in  an opportunity for its own sake society  is not a race we know one has to win  it's a it's a cooperative venture for  mutual gain so in a race it's very  important that everybody start in the  same position because the purpose of a  race is to measure relative performance  and relative talent that's not what  society is about it's just us  cooperating with one another so I think  here the question isn't can we actually  have equal opportunity but rather should  we have a political system or economic  system that tends to make sure that  people have more opportunity rather than  less and then everybody have sufficient  opportunity what I end up doing in the  book is saying that the capitalist  utopia that I described the supposed to  be juxtaposed and compared to ensuring  to be superior to the socialist utopia  that Jerry Cohen describes it endorses  principles of social justice and enjoy  endorses principles of beneficence but  the way that they achieve these goals is  not through a kind of heavy-handed  government intervention but through  private means  [Music]
 

